INSERT INTO public.game_type (name, display_name, base_shape, unit_size, description) VALUES ('t-puzzle', 'T Puzzle', '[{"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 3}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 3}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 2}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 2}, "y": {"constant": 4}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"constant": 4}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 1}}]', 70, '<p>The modern four-piece T Puzzle appeared at the end of the 19th century. The earliest known version dates from 1898 and was distributed in the United States as an advertising puzzle for Lash''s Bitters. A few years later, in 1903, another well-known version was included with White Rose Ceylon Tea, using the wordplay of arranging the pieces into a "perfect T" – or a "perfect tea."</p>
<p>The T Puzzle appears to have developed from the older five-piece <i>Latin Cross Puzzle</i>, whose known examples go back to the first half of the 19th century and are associated with Chinese origins. The four-piece T recreates the layout of the Chinese puzzle by removing the upper arm of the cross and slightly modifying the remaining pieces. Since the 1920s, the puzzle has enjoyed enduring popularity and has been repeatedly produced in wooden and plastic versions.</p>');
INSERT INTO public.game_type (name, display_name, base_shape, unit_size, description) VALUES ('f-puzzle', 'F Puzzle', '[{"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"sqrt2": 1}}, {"x": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}, "y": {"sqrt2": 1}}, {"x": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}, "y": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"sqrt2": 1, "constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 1}, "y": {"sqrt2": 2, "constant": 1}}, {"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"sqrt2": 2, "constant": 1}}]', 75, '<p>The six-piece puzzle known today as the F Puzzle is often attributed to Nob Yoshigahara, but there is strong evidence that its history goes back much further. It can be traced to U.S. Patent No. 1,630,530, filed by Richard Fryer in 1924 and granted in 1927. Fryer''s puzzle consisted of six pieces in three matching pairs and could be arranged into several geometric shapes, including the letter F.</p>
<p>Decades later, in 1987, the puzzle appeared as <i>the F</i> in Nob Yoshigahara''s book <i>Puzzle in Wood</i> and in the Hikimi Nob Yoshigahara Puzzle Collection. Yoshigahara himself linked the puzzle to Fryer''s 1927 patent. Since then, the F Puzzle has appeared in numerous editions and remains in production today.</p>');
INSERT INTO public.game_type (name, display_name, base_shape, unit_size, description) VALUES ('gem', 'Gem', '[{"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"sqrt3": 1}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"sqrt3": 2}, "y": {"constant": 1}}, {"x": {"sqrt3": 2}, "y": {"constant": 4}}, {"x": {"sqrt3": 1}, "y": {"constant": 5}}, {"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 4}}]', 57.5, '<p>The seven-piece puzzle known today as Gem is closely associated with Nob Yoshigahara, but its roots go back much further. It was inspired by an older German puzzle called <i>Ruhig Blut</i>, released in 1899 by F. Ad. Richter as No. 14 in the Anker series of mechanical puzzles. <i>Ruhig Blut</i> consisted of ten pieces based on 30- and 60-degree angles.</p>
<p>Yoshigahara simplified the set by removing three isosceles triangles, creating a new seven-piece puzzle together with a collection of 101 silhouettes. The result was Gem, which became part of the NOB puzzle series and has since appeared in numerous wooden editions.</p>');
INSERT INTO public.game_type (name, display_name, base_shape, unit_size, description) VALUES ('tangram', 'Tangram', '[{"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 4}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 4}, "y": {"constant": 4}}, {"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 4}}]', 60, '<p>Despite its reputation as an ancient Chinese game, Tangram in its modern form is actually only a little over two hundred years old. It originated in China around 1800. Its exact inventor is unknown. In China, it was known as <i>qiqiaoban</i>, a puzzle made from seven geometric pieces that can be rearranged into countless different figures.</p>
<p>Within only a few decades, the puzzle had spread to Europe and the United States, where it became extremely popular. Books filled with silhouettes of people, animals and everyday objects appeared, while puzzle sets were produced in many different materials. Its simple seven-piece design has proved remarkably enduring, inspiring generations of puzzle makers, mathematicians and players around the world.</p>');
INSERT INTO public.game_type (name, display_name, base_shape, unit_size, description) VALUES ('house', 'House', '[{"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 4}, "y": {"constant": 0}}, {"x": {"constant": 4}, "y": {"constant": 5}}, {"x": {"constant": 0}, "y": {"constant": 5}}]', 55, '<p>House is not the historical or official name of this puzzle. The game was originally introduced in Germany in 1892 by F. Ad. Richter & Co. as the <i>Cross Puzzle</i>, or <i>Kreuzspiel</i>, and became No. 10 in Richter''s series of Anker mechanical puzzles. A surviving <i>Kreuzspiel</i> problem book published in 1898 contains 133 figures to reproduce using the seven pieces.</p>
<p>Many decades later, the same puzzle appeared in Poland. During the 1970s, the Łódź-based Spółdzielnia Inwalidów "Polimer" produced it simply as <i>Łamigłówka</i> (<i>Puzzle</i>), accompanied by a collection of 133 figures – preserving even the number of challenges found in the nineteenth-century German edition. It was this Polish version that became the main inspiration for the Shape Puzzles project. Not knowing its proper name at the time, I called it House, inspired by the house-like appearance and colours of the Polish set from the PRL era. More than a century after its original publication, the same seven-piece design is still being produced under many different names (<i>Lucky Puzzle</i>, <i>Fan</i>).</p>');
